<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n106339" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὕδρα</span></b> , 
			Ion.
		
		<b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὕδρη</span></b> , ἡ, (cf. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὕδωρ</span>) <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>water-serpent,</i> but esp. of
				<i>the Lernaean hydra,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				Hes.
				<u>Th.</u>
				313
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				S.
				<u>Tr.</u>
				574
			</span></i>,<i><span class="en">
				836
			</span></i> (lyr.), <i><span class="en">
				1094
			</span></i>; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ὕδραν τέμνειν,</span> prov. of
				<i>labour in vain,</i> because two heads sprang up for every
			one which was cut off, <i><span class="en">
				Pl.
				<u>R.</u>
				426e
			</span></i>: pl., but still with reference to the Lernaean hydra, <i><span class="en">
				E.
				<u>Heracl.</u>
				950
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				<u>Ph.</u>
				[1136]
			</span></i>: prov., 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὕδρης ποικιλώτερος</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Herod.
					3.89
				</span></i>
			 (
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἐπὶ τῶν δολερῶν</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Diogenian.
					7.69
				</span></i>
			). </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>II.</b> name of a
			constellation, 
				=
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὕδρος</span>
			
			<i><span class="en">
				111
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Arat.
				444
			</span></i>, etc.</div>
